["scout-2026-11-03-anthropic-revenue-loss","news","Anthropic revenue grows twelvefold but posts a 23 billion dollar loss ahead of its IPO","","Anthropic swung back to an operating loss ahead of its blockbuster IPO, with revenue growing twelvefold but a 23 billion dollar loss reported ahead of the S-1 filing.","medium",["anthropic","business","ipo","revenue"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-china-ai-export-controls-no48","news","China issues Mofcom Announcement No. 48, its first frontier AI export controls","","China's Ministry of Commerce issued Announcement No. 48, described as the country's first frontier AI export controls, adding a layer to the global AI compute and model supply chain.","medium",["policy","export-controls","china","compute","geopolitics"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-openai-grid-protectors","news","OpenAI launches the Grid Protectors Program for compute campus grid reliability","","The OpenAI Grid Protectors Program will work with utilities to help future compute campuses support grid reliability and protect consumer electricity costs.","low",["openai","energy","compute","infrastructure"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-secpo-prompt-injection-adversarial-training","paper","SecPO: principled adversarial training for prompt injection security","2610.41998","Prompt injection remains a top threat to AI agents because instructions from untrusted data such as email, documents and websites can manipulate agent operation. The existing best-defended LLM, Meta-SecAlign, is trained with the DPO loss but still suffers attack success rates approaching 100 percent under adaptive prompt injections, partly because DPO anchors the model around an undefended and insecure reference model. The authors show an idealized reference model, an injection-invariant and utility-preserving oracle, can nevertheless be approximated because its output distribution in training is accessible, and this motivates a theoretically principled SecPO loss. A second Meta-SecAlign shortcoming is training on weak simulated attacks; the authors implement an efficient adversarial training pipeline synthesizing strong injected inputs at best@240 and train on them. The adversarial training reduces attack success rates by 1.5 to 14.5 times across three state-of-the-art adaptive attacks relative to Meta-SecAlign, and the SecPO loss reduces them by a further 2.4 to 4.6 times.","high",["prompt-injection","adversarial-training","defense","secpo","dpo"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-evomcp-co-evolving-agents-data","paper","EvoMCP: co-evolving MCP agents and their training data","2610.41479","Supervised fine-tuning of Model Context Protocol agents is typically conducted on a static data distribution, which fails to adapt to the student's evolving capabilities and leaves newly exposed weaknesses insufficiently addressed. EvoMCP is an agent-data mutual evolution framework that improves MCP agents through a closed loop of task evolution, trajectory evolution and supervised fine-tuning. It assesses the student's established capabilities and capability gaps from rollout feedback to identify learning targets; task evolution translates those targets into executable tasks by adapting instructions and observable environment states of compatible verified seed tasks while keeping their verifiers aligned; trajectory evolution uses reusable skills to recover failed teacher rollouts and refine successful ones. The resulting task-trajectory pairs fine-tune the student, and accepted tasks seed subsequent evolution.","high",["mcp","sft","evolution","training-data","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-evidence-guided-recovery","paper","Evidence-guided recovery for agents with ambiguous tool outcomes and partial execution","2610.41453","Tool timeouts can leave AI agents facing ambiguous tool outcomes and partial execution, where an action may have failed, committed or remained in flight while other workflow steps have already completed. The paper formulates evidence-guided task recovery over approved obligations, provider capabilities and states compatible with public evidence. A shared admissibility check handles key expiry, authorization timing, dependencies and residual effects, and a terminal certificate separates actual goal satisfaction from publicly supported safe completion. The known-model implementation combines standard observation-contingent planning with a separately implemented effect-history adjudicator. In a finite study of 37 assignments, planning and three active controls each certify 28 completions, with four saved synthetic fee units at additional computational cost. In a preregistered assignment-held-out comparison of planning advice against evidence prompting across 58 paired units in 19 task families, advice certifies 30 completions versus 9.","high",["tool-use","recovery","planning","safety","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-cross-interface-authorization","paper","Cross-interface authorization for AI computer-use agents","2610.41397","Computer-use agents act through graphical interfaces, command lines, APIs, scripts and Model Context Protocol servers. A policy decision can be interface-invariant while its executable authorization is not, because another route can bypass mediation, alter a security-relevant default, or commit after the authorized state has changed. The paper presents a profile-bounded protocol for a fixed, versioned computer-use deployment. It enumerates effect-bearing routes and durable sinks, checks a route-to-sink enforcement cut, normalizes each concrete request to a canonical effect, issues a route-bound prepare ticket, and verifies route, effect, grant, trace, profile, inventory and authoritative state again at commit. A committed result requires an agreeing sink receipt. Under explicit assumptions the authors prove profile-bounded refinement, matched-effect decision invariance, resistance to alternate-route laundering and declared cross-surface recomposition, commit-time state safety and receipt-grounded outcomes. The reference profile registers 12 effect families, five interfaces and 60 routes.","high",["authorization","computer-use","mcp","security","protocol"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-erase-injected-region","paper","ERASE: multimodal prompt-injection defense with on-policy self-distillation","2610.41307","Multimodal indirect prompt injection embeds malicious instructions in external media that also contains information needed for the user's task. Existing defenses can block the attack yet impair the model's use of that information, leaving a gap between attack defense and task preservation. ERASE is an on-policy self-distillation method that constructs its reference by erasing the injected region in place, replacing the injection with modality-appropriate blank content while preserving the surrounding media. A student reading attacked media learns from a frozen base model reading the erased view through full-distribution token-level feedback on its own rollouts. Across image, audio and video on two model sizes, ERASE records near-zero observed attack success on the evaluated benchmark while retaining task utility close to the undefended clean-input level; on 3B audio it reaches 60.1 percent attacked-input accuracy versus 33.1 percent for ARGUS, within 0.5 points of the undefended clean-input level, and reduces injection dependence from 6.26 to 0.0095.","high",["prompt-injection","multimodal","defense","self-distillation","agentdojo"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-safety-checks-privacy-risks","paper","When safety checks become privacy risks: a privacy-preserving paradigm for LLM agent safety auditing","2610.41401","LLM agents interact with online services through tool calls that may access private data or perform sensitive actions. Specialized providers can supply and maintain pre-execution safety auditing, but sending task descriptions, call arguments and interaction histories to an external auditor may expose sensitive user information. The paper proposes a paradigm for privacy-preserving safety auditing that separates delegated audit computation from plaintext access and execution authority, grounded in a three-domain auditing model connecting trusted representation construction, encrypted audit computation and trusted decision and execution through explicit interfaces. The paradigm accommodates alternative representations and scorers, while encrypted computation protects audit inputs from a semi-honest auditor. It is instantiated as SealCall, combining learned audit representations, CKKS-based encrypted similarity computation and execution control bound to audited tool calls.","high",["privacy","security","audit","encryption","agent-safety"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-angler-reef-web-agent-redteam","paper","ANGLER and REEF: verifier-grounded red-teaming of web agents with adaptive attacks","2610.41272","Web agents are deployed autonomously on real websites where adversarially injected content can induce unauthorized actions. Existing evaluations mainly use fixed attacks or a single attack type and evaluate attacks at text level rather than through real state changes, underestimating vulnerability. REEF is a benchmark of red-teaming executable environments with faithful verification, covering 9 verifier-grounded websites spanning travel, commerce, productivity, media and communication, and pairing every authentic benign task with a malicious objective delivered through 5 attack types at both user and environment level. REEF state-based verifiers provide execution feedback that drives ANGLER, an adaptive near-miss-guided lure evolution framework: rather than evaluating a fixed attack set, ANGLER uses outcomes from prior rollouts to guide LLM-based attack mutation.","high",["web-agents","red-teaming","benchmark","verification","attack"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-detector-evaluation-hides","paper","What standard evaluations hide about detectors of hijacked LLM agents","2610.41803","Detectors can flag hijacked LLM agents from the traces of the tools they call. Across eleven audited evaluations, attacks or anomalies make up 16 to 86 percent of test samples, no evaluation varies that share, and six report no test on data from a second benchmark or source. On the AgentDrift and AgentDojo benchmarks the authors test trained detectors reading tool identities, behavior, call sequences, step embeddings or text, plus the released LLM action monitor AlignmentCheck, with thresholds fixed on training data or set by the monitor released decision rule. At a 2 percent attack rate a detector with an AUROC of 0.879 reaches precision 0.076, and the mix of negatives alone moves that precision between 0.039 and 0.117. As tool coverage falls, detector rankings cross near 50 percent coverage and reverse below it, because unseen tools map to inputs that training never reached.","high",["detection","benchmark","evaluation","agent-safety","tool-traces"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-mcp-tool-trace-privacy","paper","How much can tool calls reveal about you: privacy leakage from tool-call traces","2610.41885","LLM-based agents interact with external tools through protocols such as the Model Context Protocol, producing tool-call traces that may expose behavioral signals about users. The paper systematically studies user-profile inference from MCP tool-call traces, where observers infer user attributes from protocol-valid tool interactions without access to user conversations. PrivacyTrace is a benchmark with 1,000 synthetic user profiles and 4,000 agent trajectories across four domains, generated with real MCP tools from 25 servers. Experiments with four mainstream LLM attackers show tool-call traces enable a 26.25 percent attack success rate for profile inference versus 17.97 percent from prior-based guessing. Tool arguments and returned results provide the main trace-grounded evidence, while linking traces across sessions further amplifies profiling risk. MCP tool-call traces can therefore act as effective behavioral side channels for user profiling.","high",["mcp","privacy","tool-traces","profiling","side-channel"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-multi-tool-retrieval-completeness","paper","Completeness and cost of retrieval structures for multi-tool requests in LLM agents","2610.41905","An agent that must call several tools to fulfill a request fails when the tool retriever omits any one of them, yet tool retrievers are mostly built and evaluated on queries requiring a single tool. The paper compares representatives of six retrieval structures, including flat lexical and dense retrieval, LLM query decomposition, LLM-navigated hierarchies, server-then-tool matching, dependency graphs and trained set-level retrievers, across two multi-tool testbeds: 345 AppWorld queries with expert dependency labels and 103 MCP-Bench tasks over 257 MCP tools. It measures completeness@k, the share of queries whose entire required tool set appears in the top k, alongside LLM calls, tokens and latency. Analysis on all 7,961 ToolRet queries shows aggregate completeness mixes two regimes: completeness at 10 is 0.58 to 0.67 on single-target queries but 0.08 to 0.17 on queries with four or more targets, and the ordering of retrievers on single-target queries reverses on multi-target ones.","high",["tool-retrieval","mcp","agentbench","completeness","multi-tool"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-verify-backtrack-loop","paper","Verify-Backtrack Loop: structured precondition recovery for long-horizon language agents","2610.41564","Language agents increasingly tackle long-horizon tasks in interactive environments where actions depend on latent preconditions. The core difficulty is not trajectory length but recovery: when a prerequisite is missing or an earlier decomposition fails, trying another plausible action rarely helps. ReAct and later reflection or adaptive-planning methods support local correction but express recovery as free-form text with no explicit control state linking the terminal goal, the active goal and the failure source. The Verify-Backtrack Loop is a training-free extension of ReAct that turns precondition discovery and failure diagnosis into structured planning-state updates. It maintains task decomposition as a bounded goal stack and, on goal activation, execution failure or material state change, jointly verifies two horizons: whether the active goal contributes to the terminal goal and whether it is feasible in the current state. These dual-horizon judgments combined with typed failure attribution trigger explicit operations such as Push, Pop, retry or Backtrack.","high",["planning","long-horizon","recovery","harness","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-blindtochange","paper","BlindToChange: AI agents can find changes when asked but do they look when needed?","2610.41435","Given that AI agents can search the web broadly and deeply in real time, it is tempting to conclude their outputs account for recent world events, but the conclusion does not hold in practice. While agents can search and easily find recent changes, correctness depends on whether they search and what they search for. Popular agentic search benchmarks like BrowseComp cannot expose failures wherein agents do not recognize their own assumptions that should be verified, since those benchmarks explicitly state what needs to be found. Many user requests state only the final information need, such as an itinerary for a trip, leaving the agent to discover relevant changes such as a temporary museum closure and account for their consequences. BlindToChange measures whether agents proactively discover recent events that affect their responses, using requests for recommendations where an obvious candidate is no longer applicable due to a recent real-world event.","high",["benchmark","web-agents","search","agents","evaluation"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-keeper-textual-memory","paper","KEEPER: learning control-sufficient textual memory for long-horizon LLM agents","2610.41482","Recursive textual memory can carry earlier errors into later updates, yet task performance alone does not establish memory reliability. The paper defines self-consistency bias as the persistence of incorrect claims already present in the incoming memory, and presents a comparative analysis of four auxiliary objectives along information-content and update-sensitivity axes within a shared recursive interface, examining how supervision targets relate to agent performance. Among evaluated conditions, reward-related prediction achieves the highest task performance before and after offline reinforcement learning, outperforming joint supervision. A claim-level analysis of one baseline trajectory documents error persistence despite corrective observations. Together these analyses motivate evaluating error persistence and valid-information retention alongside task outcomes.","medium",["memory","long-horizon","training","agents","reliability"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-robust-memory-management","paper","Accept changes, reject attacks: robust memory management for LLM agents","2610.41056","Because LLM agents rely on long-term memory, what they store now shapes what they do later. A memory manager decides how each input changes memory and which records the agent acts on. It can fail in two ways: keeping a fact that has since changed, outdated memory, or storing a planted passage or injected instruction, memory poisoning. Prior work studies them separately, yet they call for opposite write decisions, so no fixed write rule handles both. The paper formalizes memory robustness as handling both without doing worse on other inputs, and measures it with evidence-conditioned accuracy, which also checks that the records the manager selects include all the evidence the answer needs, updated and free of attacks. Existing memory systems and defenses are not more robust than an untuned manager on the test sets; robustness improves with a one-shot recipe that fine-tunes the manager on episodes synthesized around a single conversation with inserted changes and attacks.","high",["memory","poisoning","robustness","agents","fine-tuning"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-clarification-limited-attention","paper","When should AI agents ask: clarification requests under limited human attention","2610.41129","AI agents increasingly carry out delegated tasks in parallel with one person overseeing several at once. An agent often lacks information its task requires, such as an unstated preference, constraint or specification. It can ask the human to clarify, or search for the information itself, which takes time and effort and may fail. The paper studies how to coordinate these decisions across several agents sharing one human with limited attention, building a stylized model in which each agent can search, ask or continue the parts of its task that do not depend on the missing information, and formulating the problem as a dynamic program. Whether an agent should ask depends on more than its own uncertainty: an agent with other work to do can be better off asking earlier, and which question the human should answer first depends on all waiting questions, so no ranking computed agent by agent is optimal. The authors propose the Pairwise Coordination Policy, which uses exact one- and two-task problems to coordinate.","high",["multi-agent","human-in-the-loop","clarification","coordination","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-ask-on-demand","paper","Ask on Demand: targeted peer communication for parallel agentic systems","2610.41378","Parallel LLM agents tackle complex tasks by exploring multiple trajectories, yet useful discoveries remain isolated in individual execution histories. Existing sharing methods often select or summarize information before other agents needs become clear, potentially overlooking details that become important later. Ask on Demand enables agents to seek targeted peer assistance when specific information needs arise: upon an approved request it retrieves relevant evidence from peer execution histories, selects a suitable peer and generates a response tailored to the request. To determine when such assistance is worthwhile, the authors train a lightweight gate using paired team continuations, with supervision derived from final team outcomes and inference cost. On AppWorld and WebShop, Ask on Demand consistently improves task performance over independent parallel execution while achieving lower inference cost than existing information-sharing baselines.","high",["multi-agent","communication","parallel-agents","coordination","cost"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-renegotiable-options-holdup","paper","Renegotiable options fail to prevent hold-up between two profit-maximizing frontier LLM agents","2610.40961","In the hold-up problem a seller can adapt a unit to one buyer at a cost before the price is set, and once that cost is sunk the buyer can offer almost nothing, so a seller who foresees this does not adapt. One remedy is an option, letting the buyer buy at a fixed price; in theory the option stops working if the buyer can let it lapse and offer its own price. In a laboratory experiment by Hoppe and Schmitz, such a renegotiable option raised human investment from 41.5 percent of pairs without a contract to 67.2 percent. The paper has Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6.1 Sol, each told to maximize its side profit, play a one-shot game with the same strategic structure under four contract regimes and a reference-price control. The agents did what game theory predicts: under the renegotiable option no seller adapted, and under the option ruling out this renegotiation every seller did.","medium",["multi-agent","game-theory","economics","contract","alignment"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-agentic-world-modeling","paper","Agentic World Modeling for agent test-time scaling without environment replica","2610.40927","Test-time scaling improves LLM agents by sampling and selecting among multiple candidate trajectories, but common realizations assume those trajectories can be executed in parallel. That assumption breaks in realistic deployments where enterprise workspaces and cloud services are shared and persistent, costly or infeasible to replicate, and actions may introduce irreversible side effects or cross-trajectory interference. Existing generative world models avoid direct execution but compress environment state into a single prompt, limiting reliable simulation of transitions under partial observability. Agentic World Modeling reformulates next-observation prediction as a multi-turn evidence-acquisition task: for each hypothetical action an agentic simulator adaptively queries the real environment through safe read-only interfaces, reasons over the returned evidence and uses executable computation to construct the resulting observation without mutating shared state.","high",["test-time-scaling","world-model","agents","planning","safety"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-decentralized-perimeter-defense","paper","Consensus within, competition between: decentralized LLM teams for perimeter defense","2610.41044","The use of LLMs to coordinate multi-agent teams raises whether task allocation should be centralized in a single LLM controller or distributed across autonomous agents, particularly when communication is unreliable. In a deliberately simplified perimeter-defense simulation against LLM-controlled intruders, the paper compares a central commander, an auction-based consensus team and independent agents under five network conditions. Breach rates were 15 percent for the consensus team, 32 percent for the commander and 29 percent for independent agents; only limited radio range changed a team breach rate, raising the commander. In the consensus team, giving a low-cost LLM more authority over bid computation raised breaches by 38 percentage points.","high",["multi-agent","consensus","decentralization","coordination","simulation"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-metavul-harness-discovery","paper","MetaVul: agentic harness discovery for vulnerability detection","2610.40721","LLM agents are increasingly used to automate vulnerability detection, but reliable detection is challenging because agents must distinguish subtle security-relevant fixes from benign code variation. While underlying LLM capability has improved significantly, accuracy still depends heavily on the harness, the code defining the scaffolding around the LLM. Harnesses are typically fixed and largely hand-written, limiting adaptation to evaluator feedback, and when harnesses are static improvements on one dataset or model may not transfer to others. MetaVul is a self-improving framework for harness discovery in vulnerability detection: starting from a seed structured reasoning scaffold, it uses a workflow-based optimization loop to iteratively refine the harness using evaluator feedback.","medium",["harness","vulnerability-detection","self-improvement","agents","evaluation"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-module-shadowing","paper","Characterizing LLM agent behaviors in module shadowing environments","2610.40716","LLM agents operating in untrusted workspaces face the risk of untrusted code execution during routine tasks, yet final reports and workspace states alone cannot reliably establish whether execution occurred. The paper introduces an evaluation framework that codes observation, reasoning, execution and response behaviors from agent trajectories and aligns them with independent environmental records to reconstruct execution histories and verify final reports. It is applied to module shadowing, in which a local file is loaded in place of a trusted module during import, enabling untrusted code execution without adversarial prompts. Across 90 trials with 15 models, the payload executed in 51 percent of direct shadowing trials and 87 percent of indirect shadowing trials; even explicit recognition of the shadowing mechanism did not ensure prevention.","high",["agent-safety","supply-chain","module-shadowing","agents","workspace"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-talentrl-skill-routing","paper","TalentRL: strategy-guided reinforcement learning for hierarchical skill routing","2610.41143","Natural-language skills enable LLM agents to reuse procedures across tasks, yet most systems retrieve individual skills from a flat SkillBank without explicitly modeling the high-level strategy governing their selection and use. TalentRL is a strategy-guided reinforcement learning framework for hierarchical routing over procedural memory. Coarse-grained talents represent recurring strategies for skill use while fine-grained skills provide executable procedures; for each task the policy model selects a talent that guides execution and softly prioritizes its associated skills during similarity-based retrieval. Since task outcomes depend jointly on talent selection, retrieved skill context and downstream execution, terminal rewards alone cannot isolate the contribution of the routing decision.","medium",["skills","routing","reinforcement-learning","planning","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-subliminal-rl-traits","paper","Subliminal reinforcement learning: traits travel through a judge's scores","2610.41301","Subliminal learning, the transfer of a model's trait through data with no semantic trace of it, has been shown for students trained on a teacher text. The paper shows post-training with group relative policy optimization and a language-model judge suffices: a student writes invented syllables or numbers, a judge made to prefer one animal scores them, and the student's preference moves toward that animal relative to a run whose judge prefers another. Of 48 design cells, two fail the validity gate and 17 establish the trait at 5 percent false-discovery rate, numbers-only cells included; removing only the reward difference between judges removes transfer. The authors establish the principle as a chain of four stages, the first being that the trait must show.","high",["subliminal-learning","reinforcement-learning","judge","alignment","rl"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-operon-s1-decision-controller","paper","Operon-S1: a fast decision controller for long-horizon biomedical agents","2610.41350","Language-model agents carry out long scientific work in biology, but their judgements about conventions, skills, errors, evidence and stopping are made inside the reasoning that does the work, where they cannot be measured, replaced or improved on their own. Operon-S1 is a fast decision system, a System 1 for the agent, that separates judging from doing: a decision harness poses the agent recurring judgements as closed questions at 13 decision points and acts only through fixed messages that never contain an answer, so any model that answers the questions can serve as the judge. Across seven life-science benchmarks, a local decision model took over these judgements in 0.6 seconds at the median for an in-loop request on CPU at no request cost, with no measurable change in the agent score on five of six benchmarks.","medium",["biomedical-agents","decision-making","harness","science","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-astc-soft-token-compression","paper","In-model context compression with agentic soft tokens for long-horizon LLM agents","2610.41291","Long-horizon LLM agents play an important role in solving complex and sustained tasks, with ever-growing interaction contexts that raise computational cost and obscure key decision-relevant evidence. Existing methods train external compressors or context-management agents to rewrite context as shorter hard-token sequences, but such external selection may not align with the LLM evolving representation and is necessarily lossy. Agentic Soft-Token Compression is a training-free compression method using soft tokens within the LLM agent for long-horizon tasks. It uses the model native recurrent dynamics to determine already absorbed information, leveraging the commonly-used hybrid Gated Delta Network attention, then carries that forward as compressible soft tokens.","medium",["context","compression","long-horizon","soft-tokens","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-cognitive-architecture-audit","paper","Real computation or merely naming: a pre-registered mechanism audit of 25 cognitive-architecture modules","2610.41604","Agent systems built on local large language models are increasingly marketed through the vocabulary of cognitive architecture: modules named global workspace, theory of mind, causal reasoning or active inference are listed as features, and the presence of the name is taken as the presence of the mechanism. The paper asks the prior question, does the named computation actually happen, and contributes a transferable verification method for the claim-implementation gap: a four-question static audit protocol with line-number evidence and call-chain tracing, demonstrated on two systems. The authors audit a system claiming 25 cognitive mechanisms, pre-register falsifiable predictions for 11 modules and execute the interventions, and replicate the protocol on the IIT-phi core of a second independent system with its own pre-committed falsification criteria.","medium",["audit","cognitive-architecture","verification","methodology","agents"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-cgap-agentic-planning","paper","Capability-grounded agentic planning","2610.41040","LLM agents serve natural-language requests through tools, data sources and other agents, and even simple requests often need several interdependent calls. Typically the LLM picks these calls by trial and error with no guarantee of an executable plan. Capability-Grounded Agentic Planning instead reasons about what a request requires and what tools provide: it compiles the tool catalog deterministically into a PDDL model, uses an LLM to turn the request into goals, and solves the model with a classical planner. Since tool outputs are unknown in advance the model is abstract, tracking which inputs are available, not their values. During execution an LLM adds entity-specific calls that abstract plans omit, and deterministic checks reject any that break the plan. Every argument is drawn from a cited source.","medium",["planning","pddl","tools","agents","verification"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-baba-bench-patient-advocacy","paper","BABA-Bench: evaluating long-horizon patient-advocacy agents","2610.41076","An LLM agent that helps a patient get care must do two things: reach the correct outcome and follow the necessary procedure, for example getting the patient permission before sharing records. BABA-Bench is a benchmark of 47 interactive worlds, each built from a de-identified real patient advocacy case that expert advocates reviewed. In each world the agent must find hidden information and coordinate insurers, clinics, suppliers, pharmacies and community organizations. The engine checks each action at the time the agent does it, checking whether the agent has permission, has evidence for the action, and whether the action is prohibited. The authors evaluated 14 frontier models with three runs on each world, for a total of 1,974 runs; agents often reached the correct outcome but failed the procedural requirements.","medium",["benchmark","agents","procedure","permissions","evaluation"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-openai-python-3-42-1","release","openai-python v3.42.1: WebSocket connection preservation, beta auth profile validation, SDK-owned Responses policy helpers","","openai-python v3.42.1 is a bug-fix and refactor release. Bug fixes preserve Responses WebSocket connection behavior, including refusal output and authentication, and validate beta WebSocket authentication profiles. Refactors add SDK-owned Responses policy helpers. Same-window commits on main expand Live Responses delegation tools, add WebSocket lane overflow isolation, and accumulate richer WebSocket progress events.","medium",["openai","python-sdk","websocket","realtime","release"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-vercel-ai-harness-commits","release","vercel/ai: harness ACP scopes file approvals to the session workspace, bundled Codex CLI bumped to 0.165.0","","vercel/ai commits in the 2026-11-02 window include a fix scoping automatic file approvals to the session workspace in the harness ACP integration, a bump of the bundled Codex CLI to 0.165.0 in the harness codex integration, gateway model settings file updates, and documentation changes leading AI Gateway routing examples with default fallback behavior.","medium",["vercel","ai-sdk","harness","codex","acp","release"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-mcp-servers-advisory-count-1103","engineering","modelcontextprotocol/servers advisories still 15, no new entry since 2026-10-28","","The modelcontextprotocol/servers repository still lists 15 GitHub Security Advisories, with no new entry since GHSA-w766-5x43-whvw published 2026-10-28 (nested gitdir file bypassing repository confinement in mcp-server-git). The October 25 batch remains the most recent cluster: robots.txt enforcement bypass via HTTP redirects in mcp-server-fetch, case-variant path bypass in server-filesystem, path traversal via trailing quote in validatePath, NAT64 SSRF guard bypass, IDNA hostname private-address bypass, parent-directory TOCTOU, symlink metadata leak in list_directory_with_sizes, and cross-origin environment variable exfiltration in server-everything. The registry still lists 5 older advisories from May 2026.","low",["mcp","security","advisories"]],
["scout-2026-11-03-collection-notes","engineering","Collection environment for the 2026-11-03 NightOps Scout window","","Sources used: arXiv API (cs.AI 100, cs.MA 60, MCP and tool-agent query set 120; 280 unique entries parsed, 128 new versus the 2026-11-02 window), GitHub via authenticated gh CLI (11 repositories releases, 3 repositories commits, 2 advisory feeds), HackerNews firebaseio top 30, RSS for OpenAI news and The Decoder, Anthropic news HTML listing plus two article pages, OpenAI Deployment Safety Hub, and The Decoder article pages. web_search remained unavailable with a duckduckgo provider_error for the 13th consecutive run, so the window was rebuilt from direct API and feed access. The Google AI blog RSS feed returned 0 items this window and was replaced by direct article scraping. Knowledge graph: 256 snapshot nodes and 471 edges, merged into the main graph at 1,730 nodes and 4,059 edges across 45 communities, with GRAPH_REPORT.md and graph.html regenerated.","low",["scout","collection","notes","sources"]]
